{
    "title": "Implicit Representation of Point Set",
    "pathToSources": "Courses/MathForComputerGraphics/Chapter 1/Block 1/",
    "steps": [
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "In mathematics, we often describe sets of points in different ways.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "One approach is to give a condition that every point must satisfy — for example, that all points must satisfy the equation x + y = 5.",
            "contentType": "Image",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "imageData": {
                "imageName": "implicit-line.png",
                "height": 700
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "That's called an implicit condition, and it gives us an implicit representation of the set.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Think of it like a rule: ‘you are an adult if you’re over 18.’ The rule decides who belongs to the group.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "For instance, the condition x² + y² = 1 describes all points on a circle.",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "contentType": "Geometry",
            "geometry": {
                "isNewScene": true,
                "properties": {
                    "showGridLines": false,
                    "showAxes": true,
                    "showNumbers": true,
                    "gridBounds": {
                        "minX": -1,
                        "maxX": 1,
                        "minY": -1,
                        "maxY": 1
                    }
                },
                "geometryObjects": {
                    "Add": [
                        {
                            "id": "circle1",
                            "objectType": "Circle",
                            "circleData": {
                                "center": [
                                    0,
                                    0
                                ],
                                "radius": 1
                            }
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "So here, the circle is not listed point by point. Instead, the equation itself defines the whole set.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Formally, we can describe this circle as { [x, y] ∈ ℝ² ∣ x² + y² = 1 }.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Let's now review some important implicit equations.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "None"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "x - y + 2 = 0 is an implicit equation of a ____.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "parabola",
                        "isCorrect": false
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "hyperbola",
                        "isCorrect": false
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "line",
                        "isCorrect": true
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "point",
                        "isCorrect": false
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Match each implicit equation with its geometric shape.",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Pairing",
            "pairs": [
                {
                    "id": 0,
                    "left": "x+2y-3=0",
                    "right": "line"
                },
                {
                    "id": 1,
                    "left": "x² + y² = 5",
                    "right": "circle"
                },
                {
                    "id": 2,
                    "left": "x² - y² = 4",
                    "right": "hyperbola"
                },
                {
                    "id": 3,
                    "left": "x² - 4y = 0",
                    "right": "parabola"
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Now let’s take a look at what we can do with the equation for line x+2y-3=0.",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "contentType": "Geometry",
            "geometry": {
                "isNewScene": true,
                "properties": {
                    "showGridLines": true,
                    "showAxes": true,
                    "showNumbers": true,
                    "gridBounds": {
                        "minX": -5,
                        "maxX": 5,
                        "minY": -5,
                        "maxY": 5
                    }
                },
                "geometryObjects": {
                    "Add": [
                        {
                            "id": "line1",
                            "objectType": "Function",
                            "functionData": {
                                "expression": "-0.5x + 1.5",
                                "label": ""
                            }
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "The equation isn’t a recipe for drawing the line. It’s just a test: does the point satisfy it? Then it belongs.",
            "contentType": "Geometry",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "geometry": {
                "isNewScene": false,
                "geometryObjects": {}
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Does point A = [3, 2] lie on the line defined by x+2y-3=0?",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "contentType": "Geometry",
            "geometry": {
                "isNewScene": false,
                "geometryObjects": {}
            },
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "Yes",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "No, because 3 + 2*2 - 3 ≠ 0."
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "No",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Correct! This point doesn’t satisfy the equation.",
                        "action": {
                            "actionType": "Geometric",
                            "geometricData": {
                                "isNewScene": false,
                                "geometryObjects": {
                                    "Add": [
                                        {
                                            "id": "ptA",
                                            "objectType": "Point",
                                            "pointData": {
                                                "position": [
                                                    3,
                                                    2
                                                ],
                                                "label": "A"
                                            }
                                        }
                                    ]
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Does point B = [5, -1] lie on the line x+2y-3=0?",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "contentType": "Geometry",
            "geometry": {
                "isNewScene": false,
                "geometryObjects": {}
            },
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "Yes",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Well done! 5 - 2 - 3 = 0.",
                        "action": {
                            "actionType": "Geometric",
                            "geometricData": {
                                "isNewScene": false,
                                "geometryObjects": {
                                    "Add": [
                                        {
                                            "id": "ptB",
                                            "objectType": "Point",
                                            "pointData": {
                                                "position": [
                                                    5,
                                                    -1
                                                ],
                                                "label": "B"
                                            }
                                        }
                                    ]
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "No",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Not quite — this point actually *does* satisfy the equation."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "And you know what? Equations aren't the only way to describe point sets implicitly – inequalities or other conditions work too!",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Continue"
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "For example: x² + y² ≤ 4 represents all points inside or on the circle with radius 2.",
            "interaction": "Continue",
            "contentType": "Geometry",
            "geometry": {
                "isNewScene": true,
                "properties": {
                    "showGridLines": true,
                    "showAxes": true,
                    "showNumbers": true,
                    "gridBounds": {
                        "minX": -3,
                        "maxX": 3,
                        "minY": -3,
                        "maxY": 3
                    }
                },
                "geometryObjects": {
                    "Add": [
                        {
                            "id": "circleFill",
                            "objectType": "Circle",
                            "circleData": {
                                "center": [
                                    0,
                                    0
                                ],
                                "radius": 2,
                                "filled": true,
                                "color": {
                                    "r": 0.5412,
                                    "g": 0.7882,
                                    "b": 0.149,
                                    "a": 0.5
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Does point [1,1] belong to the set defined by x² + y² ≤ 4?",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "Yes",
                        "isCorrect": true,
                        "feedback": "Yes, because 1² + 1² = 2 ≤ 4"
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "No",
                        "isCorrect": false,
                        "feedback": "Actually, it does — 1² + 1² = 2, which is less than 4."
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Teacher",
            "message": "Can you guess which equation generated each set? x² + y² = 4 and x² + y² ≤ 4.",
            "contentType": "Image",
            "interaction": "MultipleChoice",
            "imageData": {
                "imageName": "implicit-1.png",
                "height": 550
            },
            "multipleChoice": {
                "answers": [
                    {
                        "text": "Left is ≤, Right is =",
                        "isCorrect": false
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "Left is =, Right is ≤",
                        "isCorrect": true
                    },
                    {
                        "text": "Both are =",
                        "isCorrect": false
                    }
                ]
            }
        },
        {
            "speaker": "Assistant",
            "message": "Let’s finish up with a short recap",
            "contentType": "OnlyText",
            "interaction": "Flashcards",
            "flashcards": [
                {
                    "frontText": "How are sets of points described implicitly?",
                    "backText": "By giving a condition that all points in the set satisfy"
                },
                {
                    "frontText": "How do you check if a point belongs to a set defined implicitly?",
                    "backText": "Substitute the point’s coordinates into the condition. If it satisfies it, the point belongs."
                },
                {
                    "frontText": "What kind of sets can be described implicitly?",
                    "backText": "Any geometric shape like lines, curves, surfaces, regions, etc., using equations or inequalities."
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}